Task 1: Deploy the Velero Operator
Subtask 1.1: Install the OADP Operator
First, we'll install the OpenShift APIs for Data Protection operator, which includes Velero.

Access the OpenShift Web Console
Log into your OpenShift cluster using the provided credentials:

oc login --server=https://api.your-cluster.com:6443 --username=admin --password=your-password
Create the OADP Namespace
Create a dedicated namespace for OADP operations:

oc create namespace openshift-adp
Install the OADP Operator
Create the operator subscription file:

cat << EOF > oadp-operator-subscription.yaml
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: redhat-oadp-operator
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  channel: stable-1.2
  name: redhat-oadp-operator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
EOF
Apply the subscription:

oc apply -f oadp-operator-subscription.yaml
Verify Operator Installation
Check if the operator is successfully installed:

oc get csv -n openshift-adp
Wait for the operator status to show Succeeded.

Subtask 1.2: Configure Storage Backend
For this lab, we'll configure AWS S3 as our backup storage backend.

Create AWS Credentials Secret
Create a credentials file for AWS access:

cat << EOF > credentials-velero
[default]
aws_access_key_id = YOUR_ACCESS_KEY_ID
aws_secret_access_key = YOUR_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
EOF
Create the secret in OpenShift:

oc create secret generic cloud-credentials \
  --namespace openshift-adp \
  --from-file cloud=credentials-velero
Create S3 Bucket Configuration
Create the DataProtectionApplication resource:

cat << EOF > dpa-config.yaml
apiVersion: oadp.openshift.io/v1alpha1
kind: DataProtectionApplication
metadata:
  name: velero-sample
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  configuration:
    velero:
      defaultPlugins:
        - openshift
        - aws
        - kubevirt
      resourceTimeout: 10m
    restic:
      enable: true
      podConfig:
        resourceAllocations:
          limits:
            cpu: "1"
            memory: 1Gi
          requests:
            cpu: 500m
            memory: 512Mi
  backupLocations:
    - velero:
        config:
          region: us-east-1
          s3ForcePathStyle: "false"
          s3Url: https://s3.amazonaws.com
        credential:
          key: cloud
          name: cloud-credentials
        default: true
        objectStorage:
          bucket: your-backup-bucket-name
          prefix: velero
        provider: aws
  snapshotLocations:
    - velero:
        config:
          region: us-east-1
        provider: aws
EOF
Apply the configuration:

oc apply -f dpa-config.yaml
Verify Velero Deployment
Check if Velero pods are running:

oc get pods -n openshift-adp
Verify the backup location:

oc get backupstoragelocation -n openshift-adp
Task 2: Backup VM Data to Cloud Provider
Subtask 2.1: Prepare Test Virtual Machine
Before creating backups, let's ensure we have a VM to work with.

Create a Test Virtual Machine
Create a simple VM for backup testing:

cat << EOF > test-vm.yaml
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: test-vm-backup
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: test-vm
spec:
  running: true
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: test-vm-backup
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Apply the VM configuration:

oc apply -f test-vm.yaml
Verify VM is Running
Check the VM status:

oc get vm test-vm-backup
oc get vmi test-vm-backup
Subtask 2.2: Create VM Backup Policy
Now we'll create a backup policy specifically for virtual machines.

Create Backup Schedule
Create a scheduled backup for VMs:

cat << EOF > vm-backup-schedule.yaml
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Schedule
metadata:
  name: vm-daily-backup
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"  # Daily at 2 AM
  template:
    includedNamespaces:
    - default
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        app: test-vm
    storageLocation: velero-sample-1
    ttl: 720h0m0s  # 30 days retention
    hooks:
      resources:
      - name: vm-backup-hook
        includedNamespaces:
        - default
        labelSelector:
          matchLabels:
            app: test-vm
        pre:
        - exec:
            command:
            - /bin/bash
            - -c
            - echo "Preparing VM for backup"
        post:
        - exec:
            command:
            - /bin/bash
            - -c
            - echo "VM backup completed"
EOF
Apply the schedule:

oc apply -f vm-backup-schedule.yaml
Subtask 2.3: Execute Manual Backup
Let's create an immediate backup for testing purposes.

Create Manual Backup
Create a one-time backup:

cat << EOF > vm-manual-backup.yaml
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Backup
metadata:
  name: vm-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
  labelSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: test-vm
  storageLocation: velero-sample-1
  ttl: 720h0m0s
  includeClusterResources: true
  snapshotVolumes: true
EOF
Apply the backup:

oc apply -f vm-manual-backup.yaml
Monitor Backup Progress
Check backup status:

oc get backup -n openshift-adp
Get detailed backup information:

BACKUP_NAME=$(oc get backup -n openshift-adp --no-headers | head -1 | awk '{print $1}')
oc describe backup $BACKUP_NAME -n openshift-adp
Verify Backup Completion
Check if backup completed successfully:

oc get backup $BACKUP_NAME -n openshift-adp -o jsonpath='{.status.phase}'
The status should show Completed.

Task 3: Restore VM from Backup
Subtask 3.1: Simulate Data Loss
To demonstrate the restore process, we'll simulate data loss by deleting our test VM.

Stop and Delete the Test VM
First, stop the VM:

oc patch vm test-vm-backup --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
Wait for the VM to stop, then delete it:

oc delete vm test-vm-backup
Verify VM Deletion
Confirm the VM is completely removed:

oc get vm test-vm-backup
You should see a "not found" error, confirming deletion.

Subtask 3.2: Perform VM Restore
Now we'll restore the VM from our backup.

Create Restore Configuration
Create a restore resource:

cat << EOF > vm-restore.yaml
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Restore
metadata:
  name: vm-restore-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  backupName: $BACKUP_NAME
  includedNamespaces:
  - default
  restorePVs: true
  includeClusterResources: true
EOF
Apply the restore:

oc apply -f vm-restore.yaml
Monitor Restore Progress
Check restore status:

oc get restore -n openshift-adp
Get detailed restore information:

RESTORE_NAME=$(oc get restore -n openshift-adp --no-headers | head -1 | awk '{print $1}')
oc describe restore $RESTORE_NAME -n openshift-adp
Subtask 3.3: Validate Restore Success
Verify that the VM has been successfully restored.

Check VM Status
Verify the VM exists and is running:

oc get vm test-vm-backup
oc get vmi test-vm-backup
Validate VM Functionality
Check VM details to ensure it matches the original:

oc describe vm test-vm-backup
Test VM Connectivity
If the VM has network access, test connectivity:

oc get vmi test-vm-backup -o jsonpath='{.status.interfaces[0].ipAddress}'
Advanced Configuration and Best Practices
Subtask 4.1: Configure Backup Retention Policies
Implement proper retention policies for your backups.

Create Retention Policy
cat << EOF > backup-retention-policy.yaml
apiVersion: velero.io/v1
kind: Schedule
metadata:
  name: vm-backup-with-retention
  namespace: openshift-adp
spec:
  schedule: "0 1 * * 0"  # Weekly on Sunday at 1 AM
  template:
    includedNamespaces:
    - default
    labelSelector:
      matchLabels:
        backup-policy: weekly
    storageLocation: velero-sample-1
    ttl: 2160h0m0s  # 90 days retention
    includeClusterResources: true
    snapshotVolumes: true
EOF
Apply the policy:

oc apply -f backup-retention-policy.yaml
Subtask 4.2: Monitor Backup Operations
Set up monitoring for backup operations.

Check Backup Statistics
Create a script to monitor backup health:

cat << 'EOF' > backup-monitor.sh
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== OADP Backup Status Report ==="
echo "Date: $(date)"
echo

echo "=== Recent Backups ==="
oc get backup -n openshift-adp --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

echo
echo "=== Backup Storage Locations ==="
oc get backupstoragelocation -n openshift-adp

echo
echo "=== Failed Backups (if any) ==="
oc get backup -n openshift-adp -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(.status.phase == "Failed") | .metadata.name'

echo
echo "=== Storage Usage ==="
oc get pvc -n openshift-adp
EOF

chmod +x backup-monitor.sh
./backup-monitor.sh
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Backup Fails with Storage Errors
Problem: Backup fails due to storage connectivity issues.

Solution:

Verify storage credentials:
oc get secret cloud-credentials -n openshift-adp -o yaml
Check storage location status:
oc get backupstoragelocation -n openshift-adp -o yaml
Test connectivity to storage backend:
oc logs deployment/velero -n openshift-adp
Issue 2: VM Restore Incomplete
Problem: VM restores but doesn't function properly.

Solution:

Check restore logs:
oc describe restore $RESTORE_NAME -n openshift-adp
Verify all resources were restored:
oc get all -l app=test-vm
Check persistent volume claims:
oc get pvc
Issue 3: Performance Issues During Backup
Problem: Backup operations are slow or timing out.

Solution:

Increase resource limits for Restic:
oc patch dpa velero-sample -n openshift-adp --type merge -p '{"spec":{"configuration":{"restic":{"podConfig":{"resourceAllocations":{"limits":{"cpu":"2","memory":"2Gi"}}}}}}}'
Adjust backup timeout:
oc patch dpa velero-sample -n openshift-adp --type merge -p '{"spec":{"configuration":{"velero":{"resourceTimeout":"20m"}}}}'
Cleanup and Resource Management
Cleanup Procedure
When you're finished with the lab, clean up resources:

Delete Test Resources
oc delete vm test-vm-backup
oc delete backup --all -n openshift-adp
oc delete restore --all -n openshift-adp
oc delete schedule --all -n openshift-adp
Remove OADP Configuration
oc delete dpa velero-sample -n openshift-adp
oc delete subscription redhat-oadp-operator -n openshift-adp
Clean Up Namespace
oc delete namespace openshift-adp
